### Why trends matter more than a single value

A single set tells you where the patient is; a trend tells you where they are **going**. A blood pressure of 110 mmHg might be normal for a young athlete and an alarming fall for a hypertensive patient normally at 170 mmHg. Trends:

- show change relative to the patient's own baseline;
- reveal gradual deterioration in which no single value crosses a threshold;
- distinguish a transient abnormality (pain, anxiety, a measurement error) from a persistent one;
- show whether treatment is working.

Plot observations on the chart, compare each set with the previous ones, and note when a patient's score has risen even if it is below the next threshold. Also remember that scores can under-read: some patients (for example those with spinal cord injuries, those on beta-blockers, or older adults) may not mount a tachycardia or fever, and a worried nurse or doctor is a valid reason to escalate regardless of the number.
